    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a>, who ended twenty years of Ming occupation in 1428, it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s longest-reigning dynasty.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a> (1460–1497) it became a Confucian bureaucratic state and conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> capital Vijaya in 1471.</li><li>From the 1530s the emperors were figureheads while the Trịnh lords ruled the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> the south.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The names of Lê-era doctoral graduates were carved on stone stelae resting on turtles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-literature-hanoi">Temple of Literature</a> from 1484; dozens of these stelae still stand there.</p></aside>
    </section>

